// The workspace exists so that the root module's TESTS compile against the
// cleat/ SDK sitting next to them, rather than against a published snapshot of
// it from the module proxy.
//
// Background. The root module must not `replace` the SDK -- `go install
// pkg@version` refuses any module whose go.mod carries a replace directive, and
// cmd/cleat, cmd/cleatctl and cmd/cleat-worker are all meant to be installable
// that way. But the root module's test fixtures legitimately import the SDK:
// testdata/ is 21 directories of workflow code written the way a user writes
// it, which is the only way to test a tool whose job is compiling exactly that.
// So the root go.mod names the SDK nowhere at all -- no require, no replace --
// and this file is what supplies it to those tests, from ./cleat in this tree.
//
// A `require` was considered and rejected. It would also work, and it would be
// worse: with one, a build outside the workspace silently resolves the SDK to
// the proxy's last published pseudo-version, and every drift test compares this
// tree against a snapshot of itself. Without one, the same build fails with a
// message naming the package it cannot find.
//
// What is NOT here is the old module cycle. The root module's PRODUCTION graph
// has no SDK dependency at all, and that is checked rather than asserted:
//
//	go list -deps ./... | grep -c cleat-team/cleat/cleat   # must be 0
//
// engine/callerrors.go, plugins/dag and cmd/cleat were each rewritten to make
// that true. This file only affects tests; `go install` ignores go.work
// entirely.
//
// TestSDKResolvesToThisTree in engine/ fails if the SDK ever resolves to
// anywhere but ./cleat, and TestRootModuleDoesNotDependOnSDK fails if a
// production package starts importing it again. Both were checked against a
// deliberately broken tree, not just asserted.
go 1.25.11

use (
	.
	./benchmarks/workflows
	./cleat
	./cleat/backendkit
	./examples
	./tests/cross-language
	./tests/plugin-harness
)
